hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  one of the key features and indeed  contributions made by Martin Heidegger's  philosophy is the emphasis on mood or  attunement which includes the the  modalities of feeling emotion all of  these sorts of things that we understand  is effectivity is absolutely central in  human existence and is having a set of  metaphysical implications and and depth  that we want to explore and this comes  up very importantly in his address what  is metaphysics about halfway through the  piece it comes up in a way to deal with  a problem that has been posed and so  that's where we begin in thinking about  this through the intellect through logic  through the sciences we've tried to  explore the nothing and we looked at  this definition nothing is the complete  negation of the totality of beings and  was it possible for us as finite  creatures to actually encompass the  totality of beings Heidegger said you  can't actually think it you can imagine  it and then think that you've actually  negated the totality of beings but you  haven't really done so another way of  putting this is can we actually  comprehend can we take in the totality  of the whole of beings in themselves not  just in an imaginary way but in an  actual way and the answer is no as  finite beings we cannot do that and we  might also say as being stationed within  the very totality we're also not able to  do that as well  so it seems like we have arrived at a  dead end in this exploration of the  nothing and here is where Heidegger  turns to this thematic of mood that is  going to get us something like a  relation to the totality of beings in a  way that the intellect itself can  do so so how does he do this he says as  surely as we can never comprehend  absolutely the whole of beings in  themselves  we certainly do find ourselves stationed  in the midst of beings that are revealed  somehow as a whole so we do find  ourselves in the middle of being and  beings and he says in the end an  essential distinction prevails between  comprehending the whole of beings and  themselves and finding oneself in the  midst of beings as a whole so the  intellect the imagination attempt to  grapple with this one modality of  comprehending and fail at doing so but  another way is through experience of  finding ourselves in the midst of being  and Heidegger then goes on and says look  the former is impossible in principle we  can't do it but the latter happens all  the time in our existence in our  everyday existence he says he says it  seems as though we cling to this or that  peculiar particular being precisely in  our everyday preoccupations as though we  were completely abandoned to this or  that region of being so when we're  thinking about say our childcare or how  we're going to do our daily commute or  whether we need to you know put  something else on the grocery list or  any of these mundane preoccupations that  seem so far away from philosophy in  general let alone metaphysics we are  actually engaged with if not the entire  totality of beings at least a an entire  region of beings when we're thinking  about questions that preoccupy ourselves  what we call existential questions that  have to do with death or birth or  trajectory or love or hate or these  other things we are concerned with  entire regions of being now does that  get us to the totality of being  Heidegger says that no matter how  fragmented our everyday existence may  appear to be it always deals with  being in a unity of the whole if only in  a shadowy way great example of this the  ties in with mood when you see somebody  posting on Facebook or on Twitter and  they've you know something bad has  happened that disrupted their day-to-day  existence and you know that that is  perhaps part of the whole cascade of  things happening and they put FML that  is a sort of global statement and it's  not necessarily them being  drama queens or you know engaging in the  kind of posting that that some people do  to call attention themselves if it's  really a genuine authentic expression of  a sort of anguish on their part they are  talking about the totality of things and  the way that the totality is pressing  upon them at that point in time  Heidegger doesn't of course use that as  an example he has some other examples  and he talks about moods or attunement  we're going to talk about certain  features of this in a moment but look at  the first example that he uses that of  boredom he says even and precisely when  we're not actually busy with things or I  solved this as the whole overcomes us  for example in genuine boredom now  notice that there could be boredom  that's not genuine boredom boredom that  is feigned boredom that is only partial  because you actually do have things to  do but I think all of us have  experienced what it's like to be  genuinely bored Heidegger is not going  to provide a detailed phenomenological  description of it here but he's  expecting us to be able to relate to it  he does tell us a few things he says  boredom is distant when it is only in  this book or that play this business or  that idleness that drags on so imagine  being stuck in some business meeting  that you know is going to be over at  five o'clock when you're going to get to  go home and and maybe do something  that's not boring or at least your trip  home will not be boring but you're stuck  there listening to people drone on and  on you're watching a PowerPoint  presentation and you're like oh this is  so mind-numbing something that many of  us have experienced many times  that is what we could call localized  boredom Heidegger is talking about a  more global boredom here he says that  it's something that erupts when one is  bored profound boredom drifting here in  there in the abyss of our existence like  a muffling fog removes all things and  human beings and one's self along with  them into a remarkable and different the  book is boredom is a key point this  boredom reveals beings as a whole so we  like I said are not able to comprehend  the totality intellectually but we are  able to find ourselves in the midst of  things and to grasp them through this  mood of boredom they're at least  revealing a certain side of themselves a  certain possibility of themselves for us  the subject he has another wonderful  example here of joy Heidegger says that  another revelation another possibility  of this is concealed in our joy in the  presence of the design and not simply of  the person of a human being whom we love  now that might sound a little bit  confusing isn't the person the deepest  level if not for Heidegger and this is a  little bit of a technical thing so the  existence the being there of the person  who we love is is more deep less  superficial than the personality of the  person the the individual who we feel  this is love towards and so when we  encounter them and you know like for  example when you you find out something  new about your partner and you rejoice  in it or even something that you already  knew about your partner and you see it  with renewed eyes you haven't seen them  for the day and you come home and you're  so happy to see them that's exactly what  Heidegger is talking about here and that  reveals the world to us not just the  person but the entire world to us in a  different way than say does bored  or we might talk about other global  moods as well like sadness or rage or  envy any any of these sorts of things  that can pervade the entirety of our  existence  now Heidegger is going to go on and tell  us a few other things that are really  key about this so one of these is he  says being attuned lets us find  ourselves among beings as a whole and he  said that the this you know the founding  mode of attunement and this is in german  deep affinity character skimming right  or bish dimming right  these are either terms that he's going  to use quite often it allows us to grasp  the the totality of beings but not in an  intellectual way in an effective way it  can have intellectual correlates we  might say it reveals beings as a whole  and he says that this is not incidental  this is something essential to human  being to design to the kind of being  that we are so what this implies is that  we should not expect that our emotional  side is something that we ought to  simply strip away or prescind from or  abstract from if we want to get at the  real so he goes on and he says what we  call a feeling is neither a transitory  epiphenomenon of our thinking and  willing behavior right it's not just a  reflection of thinking and willing nor  simply an impulse that provokes such  behavior nor merely a present condition  we have to put up somehow or other it  could be that some some feeling some  moods actually do fit into that debt  categorization but they reveal something  deeper to us or at least they have the  potentiality to do so by you might say  globalizing and he goes on now and this  is where we get to something really  important he says when moods of this  sort bring us face to face with beings  as a whole they reveal something to us  but while they reveal they also conceal  so 